/**
 * @defgroup optimal_pump_efficiency_calculator Corrected Pump-Efficiency Estimate
 * @ingroup equipment_and_property_calculators
 * @brief Estimates pump efficiency from flow and style, with viscosity and specific-speed corrections.
 * @details
 * What efficiency does the flow/style estimate predict after accounting for the liquid's viscosity
 * and the pump's specific speed? This calculation combines the separate estimates into an efficiency
 * fraction. It supplies the average estimate used by the pump-efficiency calculation; it does not
 * measure a particular pump or establish its manufacturer's best-efficiency point.
 *
 * @heading{Final Result and Interpretation}
 * The corrected estimate is:
 * @formula{optimal-pump-efficiency-result; \eta=\left(\frac{E_{pre}}{S}\right)C_\nu-C_{N_s}}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta; Returned corrected efficiency as a fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{E_{pre}; Flow/style efficiency estimate before corrections; \percent}
 * @symrow{S; Percentage-point scale per unit fraction, 100; \percent}
 * @symrow{C_\nu; Multiplicative viscosity correction factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{C_{N_s}; Specific-speed correction as a fractional efficiency decrement; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * A result of 0.73181 means about 73.181% efficiency. The pre-correction estimate is divided by
 * 100 before applying the viscosity factor; the specific-speed decrement is then subtracted
 * from that adjusted fraction. Express both efficiency terms as fractions before subtraction; do not
 * apply either correction a second time. This estimate does not include the separate deviation
 * multiplier used to calculate the maximum pump-efficiency estimate.
 *
 * @heading{Start with the Flow and Pump Style}
 * Supply liquid flow in US gpm and select the pump style. The
 * @ref optimal_pre_pump_efficiency_calculator "flow/style estimate" provides the efficiency
 * before corrections in percent. Its piecewise curve selection and equality boundaries apply here.
 * Specified-optimal, positive-displacement and unknown native styles retain a zero pre-correction
 * estimate. A zero from those styles is a fallback, not a specified efficiency or a calibrated
 * positive-displacement model. The correction steps still execute and can produce negative results.
 *
 * @heading{Apply the Viscosity Factor in the Stated Unit Convention}
 * The viscosity relationship operates on numerical values in cSt, feet, US gpm and rpm. Expressed
 * with unit ratios to make that convention explicit, the empirical parameter is:
 * @formula{optimal-pump-efficiency-viscosity-parameter;
 *   B=26.6\frac{\left(\nu/\nu_{ref}\right)^{0.5}\left(H/H_{ref}\right)^{0.0625}}
 *                 {\left(Q/Q_{ref}\right)^{0.375}\left(n/n_{ref}\right)^{0.25}}
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{B; Empirical viscosity parameter evaluated in the stated unit convention; \unitless}
 * @symrow{\nu; Supplied liquid kinematic viscosity; cSt}
 * @symrow{\nu_{ref}; Reference viscosity of 1 cSt defining the numerical convention; cSt}
 * @symrow{H; Supplied total pump head across all stages; \foot}
 * @symrow{H_{ref}; Reference head of 1 ft defining the numerical convention; \foot}
 * @symrow{Q; Supplied liquid volumetric flow; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{Q_{ref}; Reference flow of 1 US gpm defining the numerical convention; \gallon\per\minute}
 * @symrow{n; Supplied pump rotational speed; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{n_{ref}; Reference speed of 1 rpm defining the numerical convention; \revolutionsPerMinute}
 * @symrow{26.6; Retained empirical coefficient in this unit convention; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * These reference units explain the numerical inputs; they do not add runtime conversion or new
 * fitted coefficients. The viscosity expression uses the supplied total head, while the separate
 * specific-speed calculation uses head divided by stage count. Preserve that distinction.
 * The viscosity factor is:
 * @formula{optimal-pump-efficiency-viscosity-factor; C_\nu=\min\left(1,B^{-\left(0.0547B^{0.69}\right)}\right)}
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{C_\nu; Viscosity factor capped at one; \unitless}
 * @symrow{B; Empirical viscosity parameter; \unitless}
 * @symrow{1; Upper cap preventing a viscosity efficiency increase; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.0547,0.69; Retained empirical constants; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * For a positive finite parameter below one, the uncapped factor exceeds one and the cap limits
 * it to one. Above one, the factor reduces the pre-correction efficiency. The cap is applied to
 * this factor, not to the final efficiency result.
 *
 * @heading{Subtract the Specific-Speed Decrement}
 * The @ref optimal_specific_speed_calculator "specific-speed calculation" combines speed, flow
 * and head per stage in the US customary convention. The
 * @ref optimal_specific_speed_correction_calculator "specific-speed correction" then selects
 * its pump-style curve and returns a fractional decrement. Only vertical-turbine style uses the
 * vertical correction curves; axial flow uses the nonvertical correction even though its
 * pre-efficiency curves are shared with vertical turbines. The correction's lower clamp does not
 * establish a lower or upper bound on the combined efficiency.
 *
 * @heading{Worked Example}
 * Consider an ANSI/API end-suction pump at 2000 rpm, 2000 US gpm, 137 ft total head, one stage
 * and 100 cSt kinematic viscosity. The flow/style estimate is about 83.971%.
 * The viscosity parameter is about 3.128, giving a viscosity factor of 0.87195.
 * Specific speed is about 2233.596 in the US customary convention, and its decrement is about
 * 0.000372 as an efficiency fraction. Combining the unrounded values gives:
 * @formula{optimal-pump-efficiency-example;
 *   \eta\approx\left(\frac{83.971}{100}\right)(0.87195)-0.000372
 *       \approx0.73181
 * }
 * @symtable
 * @symrow{\eta; Example corrected efficiency fraction; \unitless}
 * @symrow{83.971; Rounded pre-correction efficiency used to display the substitution; \percent}
 * @symrow{100; Percentage-point scale per unit fraction; \percent}
 * @symrow{0.87195; Rounded viscosity factor; \unitless}
 * @symrow{0.000372; Rounded fractional specific-speed decrement; \unitless}
 * @endsymtable
 *
 * The estimate is about 73.181% efficiency. At 1.107 cSt with the other inputs unchanged, the
 * viscosity factor is capped at one and the estimate is about 83.934%. Round only the final
 * displayed result; retaining full intermediate precision matters near piecewise boundaries.
 *
 * @heading{Assumptions, Arithmetic Boundaries and Inappropriate Uses}
 * Interpret the empirical model using finite, physically meaningful duty data in the stated
 * units and an appropriate pump style. Stage count remains a floating value; it is not rounded
 * or restricted to integers. No double-suction flow-halving adjustment is made.
 * The retained relationships do not establish a calibration range or prediction uncertainty.
 * They do not replace a manufacturer curve, measured pump efficiency, or an engineering review
 * of the selected pump and liquid. No specified-efficiency override is performed.
 *
 * Inputs are evaluated without validation, defaults or denominator guards. The arithmetic uses
 * fmin for the viscosity cap and fmax inside the specific-speed correction. A NaN viscosity
 * expression can therefore select the numeric cap, and a NaN correction polynomial can select
 * zero. An invalid duty can return a finite number; a finite result does not validate its inputs.
 * Zero, negative and nonfinite inputs can also yield negative, very large or nonfinite estimates.
 * There is no final clamp to zero or one. Those retained arithmetic outcomes are not valid
 * operating duties or error codes, and should not be presented as physical efficiency predictions.
 *
 * @see optimal_pump_efficiency::calculate
 */
